    {
      "id": "1uwpi8y",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Importance of Agriculture in India"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Kharif crops (Rice, Maize, Cotton) are sown with the onset of the monsoon in June-July and harvested in September-October.",
        "Rabi crops (Wheat, Barley, Mustard, Gram) are sown in winter from October-December and harvested in summer from April-June.",
        "Zaid is a short summer cropping season between Rabi and Kharif, producing seasonal fruits like watermelon, cucumber, and fodder crops.",
        "The Green Revolution transformed Indian agriculture through High Yielding Variety (HYV) seeds, modern irrigation, chemical fertilizers, and farm mechanization.",
        "Key government initiatives include PM-KISAN, PM Fasal Bima Yojana, and the promotion of micro-irrigation to secure farmers' livelihoods."
      ],
      "id": "cp3cjb9"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "yt4wqto",
      "content": "• Agriculture provides employment to a large part of India's population."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ghadjdr",
      "content": "• It contributes significantly to the national income."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ogax793",
      "content": "• It supplies raw materials to industries such as cotton, sugar and jute industries."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4080kg5",
      "content": "• It ensures food security for the country."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3m4j51j",
      "content": "• It plays an important role in India's export earnings."
    },
    {
